X2 — descriptor canonical bytes are unambiguous and self-terminating
--------------------------------------------------------------------
`DistannPayloadCoverDescriptorV1::encode` (`payload_sidecar.rs:146-167`) emits,
in order:

    u16 entry_format_version
    u16 maximum_attribute_count
    u16 attribute_count
    [u8;32] row_schema_fingerprint
    per attribute:
      u16 attnum
      u16 binary_width
      len-prefixed type_namespace
      len-prefixed type_name
      i32 typmod
      len-prefixed collation_namespace
      len-prefixed collation_name
      len-prefixed send_function
      len-prefixed receive_function

Every string is `CanonicalEncoder::put_string` = `put_len_prefixed` = u32 length
+ bytes (`canonical_wire.rs:114-124`). There is no delimiter-based or
implicit-length field, so no two distinct field tuples can produce the same byte
string. `finish()` (`canonical_wire.rs:130-139`) caps total length at
`DISTANN_MAX_CANONICAL_BYTES`.

`decode` (`:169-203`) mirrors the layout exactly, bounds `attribute_count` to
1..=16 BEFORE `Vec::with_capacity` (`:174-178`, so a hostile count cannot drive
an allocation), calls `decoder.finish("payload cover descriptor v1")` (`:194`)
which rejects trailing bytes, and then calls `validate()` (`:201`).

Round-trip canonicity: `attribute_count` is written from `attributes.len()` and
consumed as the exact loop bound, and `finish()` rejects any residue, so a
successfully decoded descriptor re-encodes byte-for-byte. `validate()` pins
`entry_format_version == 1` and `maximum_attribute_count == 16`, so those two
header fields cannot vary. Result: encode(decode(b)) == b for every accepted b.


