V9 checked against reality rather than assumed: `resolve_relation_schema`
(`row_schema.rs:63-64`) emits `''` for both collation fields when
`a.attcollation = 0`. All eleven allowlist types are non-collatable
(`typcollation = 0`), so `attcollation` is 0 and the strings ARE empty for every
resolvable cover. V9 is therefore a fail-closed gate that no legitimate cover
can trip, not a latent breakage of the feature.


X5 — schema binding is the fingerprint plus per-attribute identity
-------------------------------------------------------------------
`validate_row_schema` (`payload_sidecar.rs:212-251`):
  - re-validates the supplied row schema (`:216`) rather than trusting its
    provenance — correct now that decoded descriptors will be fed in;
  - requires `self.row_schema_fingerprint == row_schema.fingerprint()?` (`:217`);
  - for each covered attnum, requires the attribute to exist and to agree on
    dropped / generated_kind / type_namespace / type_name / typmod /
    collation_namespace / collation_name / send_function / receive_function /
    fixed_binary_width (`:222-249`).

The indexed-vector attribute cannot be smuggled into a decoded descriptor even
though `validate_row_schema` does not know the vector attnum: an `ecvector`
column is not one of the eleven `pg_catalog` names, so V10 rejects it first.
The exclusion is structural, not a denylist.

Note recorded for the feedback file: because SHA-256 fingerprint equality is
effectively injective over the same canonical row-schema bytes, the
per-attribute loop at `:222-249` is redundant defense-in-depth given `:217`.
The unit test `cover_descriptor_round_trips_and_binds_the_exact_row_schema`
mutates `typmod`, which moves the fingerprint, so the test exercises `:217`
only; the per-attribute divergence arm has no test and is not reachable without
hand-forged bytes.


X6 — compact entry codec is exactly bitmap | concatenated non-null values
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
`encode_payload` (`:253-284`):
  - requires `values.len() == attributes.len()`                     (:255)
  - `null_bytes = len.div_ceil(8)`, bitmap pre-zeroed               (:262-263)
  - NULL sets bit `1 << (index % 8)` in byte `index / 8`, appends
    no value bytes                                                  (:266)
  - non-NULL requires `value.len() == binary_width` exactly         (:268)
  - appends raw value bytes with no per-entry metadata              (:276)

`decode_payload` (`:286-328`):
  - `payload.len() < null_bytes` -> truncated-bitmap error          (:292)
  - `validate_null_bitmap(bitmap, count, ...)` enforces exact bitmap
    length AND zero padding bits                                    (:296-301)
  - walks the same bit order, `checked_add` on each offset          (:309-311)
  - `end > payload.len()` -> per-attnum truncation error            (:312)
  - `position != payload.len()` -> trailing-bytes error             (:321)

Bit order is identical on both sides (`index / 8`, `1 << (index % 8)`), so the
codec is self-inverse. `validate_null_bitmap` (`canonical_wire.rs:32-56`)
skips the padding check when `attribute_count % 8 == 0`, which is correct: at
8 or 16 attributes the bitmap has no padding bits. At 16 attributes the bitmap
is exactly 2 bytes, fully used.

No per-entry cover digest, column count, offset array, or checksum is written.
The payload is exactly what plan 001 §2 "Compact canonical entry" specifies.

258-byte arithmetic: 16 attributes x 16-byte `uuid` = 256 value bytes,
`16.div_ceil(8)` = 2 bitmap bytes, total 258 == `MAX_PAYLOAD_BYTES`
(`payload_sidecar.rs:12-15`). Attained exactly by
`resolution_binds_schema_and_attains_the_258_byte_bound`.


X7 — TID / vec_id echo corruption checks
------------------------------------------
`decode_row` (`:331-353`) rejects, in order and BEFORE any value is exposed:
  1. requested TID with `block_number == pg_sys::InvalidBlockNumber` or
     `offset_number == pg_sys::InvalidOffsetNumber`                 (:339-345)
  2. `returned_row_tid != requested_row_tid`                        (:346)
  3. `returned_vec_id != requested_vec_id`                          (:349)
then delegates to `decode_payload`. `ItemPointer` derives `PartialEq`
(`src/storage/page.rs:31`), so check 2 is a full (block, offset) comparison.
`ItemPointer::INVALID` (`page.rs:38-41`) is `(u32::MAX, u16::MAX)` and is caught
by the `InvalidBlockNumber` arm.

Because the returned TID must equal the requested TID, validating only the
requested TID is sufficient — a returned invalid TID implies a mismatch.

Gap recorded for the feedback file: arm 1 has NO test. The unit test covers
arms 2 and 3 only (`:609-621`). Separately, `decode_row` does not bound
`offset_number` by `MaxOffsetNumber`, unlike the repo's existing TID-validity
idiom at `src/am/ec_diskann/routine.rs:2268` and `src/storage/wal.rs:153`.


